WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.020
Many Bash scripts begin with simple positional arguments and gradually grow options: verbose mode, dry-run mode, output path, environment, target, or apply behavior.

2
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.000
This growth is normal, but ad hoc parsing becomes hard to reason about quickly.

3
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.380
A defensive script keeps parsing rules clear enough for maintainers to understand which inputs are accepted and which combinations are rejected.

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.620
Unknown options should usually fail closed.

5
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:34.840
Ignoring an unknown option can hide typos, stale documentation, or a caller using the wrong version of a script.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:41.820
If an option requires a value, the parser should check that the value exists and is not actually another option by mistake.

7
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.080
If two options conflict, the script should reject the combination instead of choosing one implicitly.

8
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.880
Dry-run and apply behavior deserve particular clarity.

9
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:58.700
A script should not silently switch from preview to apply mode because of a missing flag or ambiguous combination.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.080
High-impact modes should be explicit, easy to review, and hard to trigger accidentally.

11
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.740
Parsing should produce a well-defined request before any operational action begins.

12
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:14.540
Tools such as getopts can help with disciplined short-option parsing, but the larger principle is separation.

13
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.880
Parse and validate first.

14
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:17.940
Act later.

15
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.640
When parsing branches are mixed deeply into action logic, reviewers have to reason about behavior and input handling at the same time.

16
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:32.140
Keeping those concerns separate makes argument security easier to test, review, and maintain.
